\title{Afternoon Heat in Rocky Tide Pools}
\author{Mara Quill \and Dev Okafor-Lind}
\date{Draft, Coastal Ecology Reading Group}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Small tide pools on the upper shore warm quickly once the tide leaves them.
We logged water temperature in twelve pools at Gull Point every ten minutes
for a summer and found that pool depth, not surface area, best predicts the
afternoon peak. Shallow pools ran up to 6\,\textdegree C warmer than the open
water, which matters for the snails and anemones that shelter in them.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}
\input{sections/methods}

\section{Results}
Table~\ref{tab:peaks} lists the mean afternoon peak by depth class. The
warming rate follows a simple exchange model,
\begin{equation}
  \frac{dT}{dt} = k \, (T_{\text{air}} - T) + \frac{q}{\rho c_p d},
  \label{eq:exchange}
\end{equation}
where $d$ is pool depth and $q$ the absorbed solar flux.

\begin{table}[h]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lrr}
    \toprule
    Depth class & Pools & Mean peak (\textdegree C) \\
    \midrule
    Under 10 cm & 4 & 27.8 \\
    10--25 cm   & 5 & 24.1 \\
    Over 25 cm  & 3 & 21.6 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Afternoon peak temperature by pool depth.}
  \label{tab:peaks}
\end{table}

